L(355, 359, "- **Runtime terms: the canary's, for every attempt**", '''
- **Runtime terms** [proposed, NP1.10(ab)]: one decision with three written alternatives. B is proposed.
  - **What the source fixes today.** Every runtime that is not the synthetic test profile has to declare a deadline of exactly 21,600 seconds (`native_u4b_policy.py:19`, `N1_DEADLINE_SECONDS`, checked by `validate_runtime_terms`, `:53–63`) and the dollar guard "90" (`native_u4b_runtime.py:171`), which starts the binary with `--max-budget-usd 90` (N1.5; D110). A registration with other values is refused. Those are N1.5's terms for the canary.
  - **What the guard is on a subscription run.** It is a backstop against a runaway run, counted in the binary's own accounting of the run at API prices. No dollars are spent: amendment N1 records D14 as "The budget is subscription usage, plus a dollar backstop."
  - **Why v5's rule cannot stand.** v5 said every attempt runs under the canary's terms. The plan's own estimates per run, for the API arm at API prices (lines 369–376), are: CHORUS about $37 expected and $49 at caps; CM4AI $83 and $102; VOICE $105 and $127; VOICE_PEDIATRIC $96 and $118, with the VOICE record as proxy; AI_READI $162 and $191; Kids First not priced. They come from another harness, and the binary's counter is its own accounting of a native run, so they are a proxy and not a measurement. On that proxy a $90 guard is expected to stop VOICE, VOICE_PEDIATRIC and AI_READI, and CM4AI is borderline. v5's rule would lose most of the matrix.
  - **The inputs point the same way.** Per the lead's record of a helper's measurement on 2026-11-12, in bundle bytes and chunks: CHORUS 35,920 and 8; VOICE_PEDIATRIC 206,008 and 13; CM4AI 320,799 and 28; VOICE 376,446 and 22; AI_READI 430,393 and 25. CHORUS is by far the smallest, and the canary's run time says little about the others.
  - **B (proposed): two registered sets of terms, and nothing else.**
    - **The canary keeps amendment N1's terms:** a $90 guard and a 21,600 s deadline. Amendment N1 is not reopened.
    - **Every pilot after the canary and every production cell runs under one production set, fixed here:** a $400 guard and a 43,200 s deadline. $400 is about 2.5 times the largest expected estimate, AI_READI's $162; the canary's $90 is about 2.4 times CHORUS's $37. The deadline is twice the canary's. Neither value rests on a native measurement, because none exists.
    - **The source change.** Before the canary, the source is changed to accept exactly these two registered sets and nothing else. A registration declares one set whole; a mixed pair or any other value is refused. No project is named in code, and nothing infers the set from the project: the registration says which, and the review bundle and the owner's word pin the registration. This is an engineering prerequisite and is not written (above).
    - **Why the canary can still be CHORUS's pilot.** The canary and production then share one source and one set of assets (NP1.5, condition 2). They differ in a registered value that binds only when a run is stopped, and a stopped run is not accepted; only an accepted attempt supplies a fraction (NP1.5). It is a declared difference, named beside the floor's value. NP1b checks that it is no more than that: if the registered terms reach anything the model reads, the two sets differ in model-facing bytes, and under NP1.5's condition 2 the canary is then outside the production stratum.
    - **The production set is fixed before any native cost is measured.** The canary's measured cost and run time do not change it. If they show that the set cannot hold a project, that project's attempts end on the guard or the deadline and are classified like any other stop; other terms need a later amendment and a new stratum.
    - **What a longer run changes.** A 43,200 s run crosses more five-hour usage windows than a 21,600 s run. The admission terms (NP1.10(ac)) and the usage-stop class (NP1.6, row 4) already cover a mid-run exhaustion, and N1.5 says one is possible whatever the admission reading. The stand-down each attempt asks for doubles (below).
  - **A: one set for every run, the canary included:** a $400 guard and a 43,200 s deadline. The code is simplest, two constants, and the canary and production do not differ at all. But it changes two of amendment N1's values, its item (p) and the guard D110 decided, so amendment N1 is revised and balloted again before the canary.
  - **C: the canary's terms for every run,** as v5 had it. No code changes. On the plan's estimates three projects are expected to end on the guard and one is borderline. A project that needs other terms then needs source support and a later amendment, and its cells would be a new stratum with a pilot of its own.
  - **A run that ends on the deadline or the guard has no rule of its own,** under any of the three. The attempt is classified by NP1.6 like any other stop, with its mechanism recorded beside its class, and its cell and project are described by the plan's kinds of missing (NP1.2). Whether the next attempt gets a word is the owner's decision, each time (D22).
''')
